Z||000000||Zoom88||12-31-2001||02:17 PM||||Ok, I read about several persons on here using scanners to set things such as IAC counts and TPS voltage. What type of scanners are you people talking about and where can I buy one at a reasonable cost. I have a scanner that is used to read codes and reset the computer but it doesn't do any of the above mentioned !!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-31-2001||02:32 PM||||I recently got a reconditioned one from <A HREF="http://www.autocheckup.com" TARGET=_blank>www.autocheckup.com</A>  The model I got is the Auto X-Ray model XP240 for GM. I cost me $89.99 plus shipping. It will read the codes and can reset them. It provides a ton of information. I haven't had a chance to use it yet, but the owners manual was very informative. It even comes with the right GM cable to hook up to the ALDL port. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||12-31-2001||03:54 PM||||Here's a picture of mine. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Autoxray.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-31-2001||05:13 PM||||The AutoXray scanner is good and does support ECM's with the ability to reset codes thru the scanner.... I'm not aware of the Fiero ECM's having that feature. (I have the AutoXray.)<P>Scanners are a bit hard to explain...<P>It will tell you all sorts of things... any scanner will. making sense of those things is another story. The book that comes with the scanner is only a starting point.<P>In most cases to really make a scanner work for you, you'll still need other things like noid lights and a good volt/ohm meter.<P>The noid lights will tell you when the ECM is actually moving the IAC and firing the injector. The scanner is to laggy to tell you that in real time. (It's because the data stream from Fiero ECM's is kind of slow. Not because of bad scanner design.) The scanner can tell you if one or both IAC coils are on but by the time you see that info, the data can be rather stale.<P>Just an example....<P>The ECM scanner reports a TPS code because it is reading the TPS out of range at idle. Is the TPS bad or is it something else? If you have a volt/ohm meter you can double check the voltage at the TPS and see if it is really out of range. If it is then you may think the TPS is bad but in reality it could be a bent part on some modles of throttle body....<P>Other times the scanner is totally useless.<P>Another example... You've got a slight miscalibration of the MAT sensor on an 87-88 4 cylinder. The scanner will only tell you what the ECM is being told by the sensor. Problem is you have no way, without special tools, to know if that number is accurate. If the number is 20-30 degrees off then you can have a number of wierd problems crop up. Like a 45 that only sets during idle in certain cold weather conditions.<P>The scanner is useful but often only if you know how to use it well or have someone that can help you with it. It's very important to know how differant numbers are related to each other... The scanner's book usually can't tell you all that.<P>For a 87-88 4 cyl they are almost required... that ECM runs things besides the engine, like the coolant fan and the cruise control. In these models, if the car is over heating, and the fan not spinning up, the scanner will tell you if the ECM is even trying to spin it. If cruise isn't working it can quickly help determin if it's a control or a servo problem.<P>In the older 4 cyl and the stock V6 setups, sometimes it helps others it won't. All depends what is wrong.<P>In all models it will also tell you if the ECM is trying to lock the TC in an automatic.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||SKIDMARK||12-31-2001||06:48 PM||||If I understand you correctly, you must be confused! A scanner is a diagnostic tool only. You can't "set" things such as IAC counts and TPS voltages using a scanner, you can only read them. Maybe I'm the one that's confused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-31-2001||06:56 PM||||Depending on the year of the car, the ECM and the quality of the scanner, you can reset codes and diddle some things under the hood.<P>The Fiero ECM ain't one of them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||12-31-2001||09:27 PM||||I bought the scanner for troubleshooting purposes only. It will reset ECM error codes so you don't have to disconnect the battery, but it will also give you a wealth of information on the sensors it reads.||||reg||1
